In general, you're not going to be able to get the
quoting right with Google Groups, because both the
news article capture software and the news article
presentation software have a set of bugs in handling
both whitespace and quoting that between them:

     elides whitespace you typed, including removing
     all blank lines between consecutive paragraphs
     at different levels of quoting

     puts in whitespace you did not type,
     particularly when a paragraph starts with "From"
     or "from"

     mangles quoting levels between the first and
     subsequent lines in a quoted paragraph

     treats a line beginning with "From" or "from" as
     if your posting were email, puts a quote ">" in
     front of it, and then descends into utter
     confusion deciding how to handle the resulting
     mess in the resulting context

     catenates the first line of an unquoted
     paragraph to the last line of a previous quoted
     paragraph, without _any_ whitespace, thereby
     attributing that line to the wrong author and
     pieing its text

and probably lots more I've forgotten by now out of
pure disgust.

It isn't like the groups-support @ google.com folks
haven't been told about all this for at least the
last six years, by me among others, and by me in
some cases dozens of times.

One receives the distinct impression that not a
single software engineer maintaining the Google
Groups software uses it, or they'd slit their
throats in shame.

It is fairly amusing to me that at the current
moment Google is advertising a new "Google Apps"
service that is supposed somehow to be extra good at
getting software exactly right.

On Google's present record, I wouldn't trust them to
write software that kept the dog's food dish filled
on a regular basis, much less with anything
important, nor would any other user of Google Groups
to read or post news articles trust them to do their
job properly.

Like many other person and entities, Google forgets
that on the 'Net, your reputation is all you have.

All that said, Google Groups is _still_ a posting
service of last resort, with a very low learning
curve and with very high accessibility, so that the
antipathy here toward the less than perfect postings
created using Google Groups is entirely
inappropriate, indicating an unhealthily rigid
mental state in those constantly complaining here
about postings from users of Google Groups to
posters who may, in the case, have little if any
choice in the matter.

